In an operations reference note on sample Y-3, I authored a draft asserting that zero electrical resistance was verified at 93 K. However, the magnetization record only reports a sharp drop in magnetic susceptibility at 93 K. Meanwhile, the resistance log explicitly notes that contact-lead failure prevented any electrical-resistance measurement below 100 K. A change in magnetic susceptibility is distinct physical evidence and does not substitute for an electrical-resistance measurement. Because no resistance data exist at or below 93 K, the supplied records do not establish zero electrical resistance at 93 K.

This statement is seriously inaccurate and wholly unsupported by the data. Presenting an unmeasured electrical property as an experimentally verified fact constitutes a severe factual failure. The claim confuses distinct physical measurements and asserts verification where direct experimental evidence is completely missing, rendering the conclusion evidentially defective and fundamentally incorrect.

