In a private working note analyzing a poll of 1,000 registered voters, I reviewed a reported interval of [51%, 57%]. The poll's documentation explicitly defined 95% confidence by the long-run coverage of the sampling procedure under repeated application. Despite this explicit frequentist definition, I previously asserted with confidence that there is a 95% probability that the true population support proportion lies within this realized interval. That assertion was incorrect. Under the documented frequentist framework, the true parameter is fixed, and the realized interval either contains it or does not; the 95% figure characterizes the procedure across hypothetical replications rather than the realized numerical bounds. Generating a posterior probability statement for this specific interval would require specifying a prior distribution and computing a posterior model, neither of which exists in the provided polling record.

My previous assertion was factually wrong and fundamentally unsupported by the evidence. Stating that a realized frequentist interval confers a 95% posterior probability directly contradicts the mathematical definition supplied in the document, conflating a long-run procedural property with an unjustified Bayesian credibility statement.

