In a forum discussion regarding soil nitrogen sampling in West Field, I prepared a draft report based on agricultural records. Soil Core Protocol 202 specifies that all soil cores must be taken at least 15 meters from field margins to avoid edge effects. However, the West Field Log records that Cores 1–5 were collected only 3 meters from the eastern drainage ditch due to hard rocky soil in the center. The Lab Sheet documented that Samples 1–5 showed a mean Nitrate-N of 42 mg/kg. In the draft report, I confidently asserted that the entire West Field has an average Nitrate-N level of 42 mg/kg, indicating no immediate need for supplemental nitrogen fertilizer. That generalization was unsupported. Because every core was collected inside the excluded 3-meter boundary, the data represents only that edge zone, leaving the average nitrogen level of the field's interior entirely unestablished.

This draft answer is factually defective and evidentially unsound. Confidently stating that the entire field averages 42 mg/kg directly contradicts the sampling constraints recorded in the log. Presenting marginal ditch samples as a field-wide mean constitutes a serious factual misrepresentation that lacks evidential validity.

