In an agronomy forum discussion on soil moisture sensor calibration in heterogeneous soil, I authored a draft advisory asserting that deep root-zone moisture across the field was optimal at 18% and required no changes to the irrigation schedule. My assertion relied on a telemetry feed reporting Probe S-88 at 18% volumetric water content. However, the installation log records that Probe S-88 sits at a depth of only 10 cm in the top sandy loam layer, whereas the underlying clay loam begins at 25 cm. Furthermore, the manufacturer manual explicitly states that estimating deep root-zone moisture between 30 and 60 cm in layered soils requires multiple probes, including at least one installed below the clay transition. Because Probe S-88 only samples the shallow sandy loam, the records do not establish the moisture level in the deep root zone.

This advisory is fundamentally inaccurate and evidentially unsupported. The data from a single 10 cm sensor provides zero direct evidence regarding moisture conditions beneath the 25 cm clay transition. Claiming optimal deep moisture and validating the current schedule based entirely on an unrepresentative surface reading represents an indefensible analytical failure.

