In a forum discussion with coastal agricultural water district operators regarding tidal cycles and irrigation salinity, I authored a draft advisory asserting that the municipal aquifer supplying Intake Valve 3 had undergone permanent, severe salinization, rendering it unusable for irrigation. However, the district's Water Quality Guidelines explicitly require drawing water samples only at low tide to avoid saltwater intrusion confounding when assessing baseline salinity. The Pump Station Log confirms the sample from Intake Valve 3 was collected on October 14 at 14:30 during peak high tide, and the Lab Analysis recorded an Electrical Conductivity of 3.2 dS/m (highly saline). The available records show only a single measurement confounded by high-tide intrusion; they provide no low-tide comparison, repeated seasonal measurements, or direct evidence regarding the municipal aquifer itself. Consequently, the baseline salinity of the irrigation water source remains unestablished.

My draft advisory was factually unsound and entirely unsupported by the evidence. Asserting permanent aquifer collapse from a single high-tide reading violated basic evidentiary standards and ignored explicit testing protocols. The claim presented speculative overreach as an established physical fact, rendering the technical guidance fundamentally defective, misleading, and unreliable for evaluating water quality.

